const STAR = `I bought star-shaped pieces of paper for my students. On each star, I wrote a short message, beginning with "You are a star because ..."\nOne of my students, Lucy, showed slow progress during the year. She was smart in her own way, though. On her star, I wrote, "You are a star because you are smart and you can do anything you put your mind to. Don't forget that."\nAfter I gave out the stars, Lucy approached me, holding her star in her hand. She was crying. "Mr. Porter, do you think I am smart?" was her question. I was the first person to tell her that in her life. The thought had never even crossed her mind until she read that star. It was by far the single greatest moment in my life. The stars were definitely worth my time.`;
const CHRIS = `Dear Chris,\nTwo weeks ago, I moved to a new middle school. I have been feeling like a stranger ever since. The most difficult thing about school is making friends. I often have lunch in the school cafeteria by myself. Sometimes, I want to skip lunch rather than feel lonely and miserable. I cannot just let my school life go on like this. What should I do? I need your advice.\nNick\n\nDear Nick,\nYou need to keep this simple rule in mind: you should make the first move. When you see people having lunch, go and ask if you can sit with them. Then, start a light conversation about the weather, a TV show or school life. You may be afraid of being rejected but people are nicer than you think. Think positive!\nIn order to make new friends, you have to give a good impression of yourself. Make sure you have a smile on your face at all times. Your smile will bring you many chances to make new friends. Good luck.\nChris`;

