add(R, `다음 글의 (A)~(E) 중, 주어진 문장이 들어갈 알맞은 곳은?\n\nThanks to the report, however, Nobel decided to do something to contribute to the world.\n\nIn 1888, a French newspaper mistakenly reported Alfred Nobel's death. (A) The person who had actually died was his brother, Ludvig. (B) In 1895, he decided to use his money to create the Nobel Prize. (C) Originally, there were only five awards. (D) A sixth award was added in 1968. (E) Today, when we think of Alfred Nobel, we think of the Nobel Prize, rather than dynamite.`, '2', ABCDE);
add(R, passage(D, NOB1.replace('While he was drinking his coffee, a headline', 'While he was drinking his coffee, a ⓐ<u>headline</u>').replace('The Merchant of Death', 'The ⓑ<u>Merchant</u> of Death').replace('Reading the article, he dropped', '(A)<u>Read</u> the article, he ⓒ<u>dropped</u>').replace('His coffee spilled', 'His coffee ⓓ<u>spilled</u>') + `\n${NOB2.replace('Catching his breath, Nobel kept reading.', 'Catching his breath, Nobel kept (B)<u>reading</u>.').replace('The article described him', 'The article ⓔ<u>described</u> him').replace('It said that he had become rich', 'It said that he (C)<u>had become</u> rich').replace('But he never imagined that the world', 'But he never imagined (D)<u>that</u> the world').replace("He decided to change people's opinions about him.", "He decided (E)<u>changing</u> people's opinions about him.")}`.replace(/^다음 대화를/, ''), `위 글의 밑줄 친 (A)~(E) 중, 어법상 어색한 것을 모두 골라 짝지은 것은?`).replace(D, P), '2', ['(A), (B)', '(A), (E)', '(B), (C)', '(C), (D)', '(D), (E)']);
const NOB74 = NOB1.replace('While he was drinking his coffee, a headline', 'While he was drinking his coffee, a ⓐ<u>headline</u>').replace('The Merchant of Death', 'The ⓑ<u>Merchant</u> of Death').replace('Reading the article, he dropped', 'Reading the article, he ⓒ<u>dropped</u>').replace('His coffee spilled', 'His coffee ⓓ<u>spilled</u>') + `\n${NOB2.replace('The article described him', 'The article ⓔ<u>described</u> him')}`;
add(R, passage(P, NOB74, `위 글의 밑줄 친 ⓐ~ⓔ의 영영풀이로 알맞지 않은 것은?`), '5', ['ⓐ headline: the title written in large letters over a story in a newspaper', 'ⓑ merchant: a person who sells products in large amounts', 'ⓒ drop: to let something fall', 'ⓓ spill: to flow over the edge of a container', 'ⓔ describe: to help to cause something to happen']);
add(R, passage(P, NOB74, `위 글에 드러난 Alfred Nobel의 심경을 나타내는 말로 알맞지 않은 것은?`), '1', ['satisfied', 'shocked', 'disappointed', 'surprised', 'unhappy']);
add(R, passage(P, NOB74, `위 글을 읽고 답할 수 없는 질문은?`), '2', ['What did Alfred Nobel invent?', 'When did Alfred Nobel actually die?', 'Why were Alfred Nobel disappointed at the article?', "What did the world think of Alfred Nobel's inventions as?", 'What kind of person did Alfred Nobel want to be remembered as?']);
add(R, `다음 글의 괄호 (A), (B), (C)에서 어법상 알맞은 말로 바르게 연결된 것은?\n\nIn 1888, a French newspaper reported Alfred nobel's death. However, the person who (A)[has / had] actually died was his brother, Ludvig. Thanks to the report, however, Nobel decided to do something (B)[helped / to help] the world. In 1895, he decided to use his money to create the Nobel Prize. In the beginning, there were only five awards. A sixth award (C)[added / was added] in 1968. Today, when we think of Alfred Nobel, we think of the Nobel Prize, rather than dynamite.`, '4', ['(A) has – (B) helped – (C) added', '(A) has – (B) to help – (C) was added', '(A) had – (B) to help – (C) added', '(A) had – (B) to help – (C) was added', '(A) had – (B) helped – (C) was added']);
const NOB78 = `Although the person who had actually died was his brother, Ludvig, in 1888, a French newspaper mistakenly reported Alfred Nobel's death. While reading the article about his own death, Nobel realized that people would remember him as a person who threatened the world by creating dangerous objects for war. He didn't want to be remembered like that. He wanted to be remembered as a person who tried to make a better world. So in 1895, he donated his money to create the Nobel Prize. And a year after that, in 1896, Nobel died at the age of 63. Originally, there were only five awards. A sixth award was added in 1968. Today, when we think of Alfred Nobel, we think of the Nobel Prize, rather than dynamite. The story of Alfred Nobel shows that ⓐ______________________________.`;
add(R, passage(P, NOB78, `위 글의 빈칸 ⓐ에 들어갈 말로 알맞은 것은?`), '5', ['we should try not to misunderstand others', "you don't have to be remembered as a good person by everyone", 'newspaper should be careful not to report what is not true', 'it is important to forgive others for making mistakes and be generous to them', 'if you change yourself into someone different from the past, people will remember you as the person who has been changed']);
add(R, passage(P, NOB78, `위 글을 통해 추론할 수 없는 것은?`), '3', ['Before Alfred Nobel died, his brother, Ludvig, had died.', 'The article led Alfred Nobel to think about creating the Nobel Prize.', 'After his death was mistakenly reported, it took more than ten years for Alfred Nobel to donate his money for the Nobel Prize.', "The year before Alfred Nobel's death, he used his money to create the Nobel Prize.", 'For a certain period of time, there were five awards in the Nobel Prize.']);
add(R, passage(P, NOB78, `위 글을 다음과 같이 요약할 때, 빈칸 (A), (B), (C)에 들어갈 말로 알맞게 연결된 것은?\n\nIt wasn't (A)__________ that Alfred Nobel was dead as reported in newspaper in 1888. After reading the article, he thought he didn't want to be remembered as a person who made the world (B)__________. He decided to do something to (C)__________ the world.`), '2', ['(A) true – (B) better – (C) contribute to', '(A) true – (B) dangerous – (C) contribute to', '(A) true – (B) dangerous – (C) stand up against', '(A) false – (B) better – (C) stand up against', '(A) false – (B) dangerous – (C) contribute to']);
add(R, `다음 글의 밑줄 친 표현의 의미로 알맞은 것은?\n\nIt was just a normal morning. Alfred Nobel sat in his chair to read the newspaper. While he was drinking his coffee, a headline caught his eye: The Merchant of Death, Alfred Nobel, Is Dead. "What? What is this?" Reading the article, he dropped his cup in surprise. His coffee spilled all over his clothes and desk, but <u>he couldn't take his eyes off the newspaper</u>.`, '4', ['그는 신문을 살 수 없었다.', '그는 신문을 찢을 수 없었다.', '그는 신문을 읽지 않을 수 없었다.', '그는 신문에서 눈을 뗄 수가 없었다.', '그는 눈이 아파서 신문을 읽을 수 없었다.']);
const NOB82 = `The article was about his own death! It said Nobel ⓐ<u>had died</u> in France from a heart attack.\n"Oh my goodness! Am I dead?"\n(A)<u>After he caught his breath</u>, Nobel kept reading. Soon, he became ⓑ<u>even more shocked</u>. The article described him (가)________ the inventor of dynamite and other dangerous objects for war. It said that (B)<u>그가 다른 사람들의 죽음으로 인해 부유해졌다</u>. He couldn't believe his eyes. It was true that dynamite was ⓒ<u>one of his many invention</u>. But he never imagined that the world would think of him (나)________ "the merchant of death."\nNobel was ⓓ<u>deeply disappointed</u>.\n"How could this be? This is unbelievable! I'm not a merchant of death. I want to be remembered in a different way. I want to be remembered as a person ⓔ<u>who made</u> the world better."\nHe decided to change people's (다)______________ about him.`;
add(R, passage(P, NOB82, `위 글의 밑줄 친 ⓐ~ⓔ 중, 어법상 어색한 것은?`), '3', CIRCLE);
add(R, passage(P, NOB82, `위 글의 밑줄 친 (A)를 분사구문으로 알맞게 전환한 것은?`), '2', ['Caught his breath', 'Catching his breath', 'To catch his breath', 'Being caught his breath', 'To be catching his breath']);
add(R, passage(P, NOB82, `위 글의 빈칸 (가), (나)에 공통으로 들어갈 말로 알맞은 것은?`), '1', ['as', 'for', 'over', 'about', 'from']);
add(R, passage(P, NOB82, `위 글의 밑줄 친 (B)의 우리말과 같은 뜻이 되도록 <보기>의 단어 및 표현을 배열할 때, 네 번째로 오는 것은?\n\n<보기> from / had / the deaths / rich / he / of others / become`), '2', ['had', 'rich', 'become', 'of others', 'from']);
add(R, passage(P, NOB82, `위 글의 빈칸 (다)에 들어갈 말로 알맞은 것은?`), '3', ['roles', 'contents', 'opinions', 'conditions', 'characters']);
